be due to stigma, lack of access to healthcare services, and a patient's primary language being one other than English.\n\n[4] More recent CDC surveillance data show a different pattern in prevalence estimates, with ASD prevalence in recent samples being lower among White children than among several other racial and ethnic groups, including Black, Hispanic, Asian/Pacific Islander, and American Indian/Alaska Native children. However, higher observed prevalence does not mean disparities have disappeared. Instead, it may reflect improvements in recognition in some communities while major inequities remain in the timing of diagnosis, quality of assessment, comorbid condition management, and service access. In other words, being counted is not the same as being equitably served.\n\n[5] Disparities in ASD cannot be understood through gender or race alone. Gender-based underrecognition, especially among girls, and racial inequities in diagnosis and care continue to shape who receives support and who is left behind.\n\n[6] ASD occurs in all racial, ethnic, and socioeconomic groups, but its diagnosis is far from uniform across these groups [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Caucasian children are consistently identified with ASD more often than black or Hispanic children (6) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. ASD is more common in males (22,23) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf], but the true male-to-female ratio is closer to 3:1 (24) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Girls who meet criteria for ASD are at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis (24) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. The female autism phenotype may play a role in girls being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked (25) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Girls are more likely to mask their social deficits through camouflaging (25) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf]. Gender biases and stereotypes of ASD as a male disorder could also hamper diagnoses in girls (26) [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf].\n\nDRAFT ANSWER (working material — not the source of truth):\nThe research suggests that girls from minority racial groups may experience even greater disparities in ASD diagnosis due to a combination of factors, including confusion during the diagnostic process, limited culturally responsive communication, and frustration when parental concerns are dismissed, leading to later identification of symptoms [Source: learned_qa]. This may result in girls from minority racial groups being at higher risk of not receiving a clinical diagnosis, and being misdiagnosed, diagnosed later, or overlooked [Source:../data/pdfs/Autism spectrum disorder definition, epidemiology, causes, and clinical evaluation.pdf].\n\nYour task:\n- Rewrite the draft into the final answer, keeping only claims that are supported by the CONTEXT above.\n- Drop any claim in the draft that is not grounded in the CONTEXT, even if the draft stated it plainly.\n- Do not add claims beyond what the CONTEXT supports, whether or not the draft mentioned them.\n- Match the answer's length and confidence to what the CONTEXT actually supports — a thin context\n  means a short, honest answer, not a long one padded from the draft's phrasing.\n- If sources disagree, report the disagreement — do not silently pick one.\n\nOUTPUT FORMAT:\n- Plain prose, no headings, no bullet lists.\n- State coverage honestly when it is partial or